" (https://www.doc.ic.ac.uk/~ajd/Publications/newcombe_etal_iccv2011.pdf)\nciteturn288view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.doc.ic.ac.uk/~ajd/Publications/newcombe_etal_iccv2011.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 728\nL0@P0: DTAM: Dense Tracking and Mapping in Real-Time\nL1@P0: Richard A. Newcombe, Steven J. Lovegrove and Andrew J. Davison\nL2@P0: Department of Computing, Imperial College London, UK\nL3@P0: [rnewcomb,sl203,ajd]@doc.ic.ac.uk\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: DTAM is a system for real-time camera tracking and recon\u0002struction which relies not on feature extraction but dense,\nL6@P0: every pixel methods. As a single hand-held RGB camera\nL7@P0: flies over a static scene, we estimate detailed textured depth\nL8@P0: maps at selected keyframes to produce a surface patchwork\nL9@P0: with millions of vertices. We use the hundreds of images\nL10@P0: available in a video stream to improve the quality of a sim\u0002ple photometric data term, and minimise a global spatially\nL11@P0: regularised energy functional in a novel non-convex opti\u0002misation framework. Interleaved, we track the camera’s\nL12@P0: 6DOF motion precisely by frame-rate whole image align\u0002ment against the entire dense model. Our algorithms are\nL13@P0: highly parallelisable throughout and DTAM achieves real\u0002time performance using current commodity GPU hardware.\nL14@P0: We demonstrate that a dense model permits superior track\u0002ing performance under rapid motion compared to a state of\nL15@P0: the art method using features; and also show the additional\nL16@P0: usefulness of the dense model for real-time scene interac\u0002tion in a physics-enhanced augmented reality application.\nL17@P0: 1. Introduction\nL18@P0: Algorithms for real-time SFM (Structure from Motion), a\nL19@P0: problem alternatively referred to as Monocular SLAM, have\nL20@P0: almost always worked by generating and tracking sparse\nL21@P0: feature-based models of the world. However, it is increas\u0002ingly clear that in both reconstruction and tracking it is pos\u0002sible to get more complete, accurate and robust results us\u0002ing dense methods which make use of all of the data in\nL22@P0: an image. Methods for high quality dense stereo recon\u0002struction from multiple images (e.g. [15, 4]) are becoming\nL23@P0: real-time capable due to their high parallelisability, allow\u0002ing them to track the currently dominant GPGPU hardware\nL24@P0: curve. Meanwhile, the first live dense reconstruction sys\u0002This work was supported by DTA scholarships to R. Newcombe and\nL25@P0: S. Lovegrove, and ERC Starting Grant 210346. We are very grateful to our\nL26@P0: colleagues at Imperial College London for countless useful discussions.\nL27@P0: tems working with a hand-held camera have recently ap\u0002peared (e.g. [9, 13]), but these still rely on feature tracking.\nL28@P0: Here we present a new algorithm, DTAM (Dense Tracking\nL29@P0: and Mapping), which unlike all previous real-time monoc\u0002ular SLAM systems both creates a dense 3D surface model\nL30@P0: and immediately uses it for dense camera tracking via whole\nL31@P0: image registration. As a hand-held camera browses a scene\nL32@P0: interactively, a texture-mapped scene model with millions\nL33@P0: of vertices is generated. This model is composed of depth\nL34@P0: maps built from bundles of frames by dense and sub-pixel\nL35@P0: accurate multi-view stereo reconstruction. The reconstruc\u0002tion framework is targeted at our live setting, where hun\u0002dreds of narrow-baseline video frames are the input to each\nL36@P0: depth map. We gather photometric information sequentially\nL37@P0: in a cost volume, and incrementally solve for regularised\nL38@P0: depth maps via a novel non-convex optimisation framework\nL39@P0: with elements including accelerated exact exhaustive search\nL40@P0: to avoid coarse-to-fine warping and the loss of small details,\nL41@P0: and an interleaved Newton step to achieve fine accuracy.\nL42@P0: Meanwhile, in an interleaved fashion, the camera’s pose is\nL43@P0: tracked at frame-rate by whole image alignment against the\nL44@P0: dense textured model. This tracking benefits from the pre\u0002dictive capabilities of a dense model with regard to occlu\u0002sion handling and multi-scale operation, making it much\nL45@P0: more robust and at least as accurate as any feature-based\nL46@P0: method; in particular, performance degrades remarkably\nL47@P0: gracefully in reaction to motion blur or camera defocus.\nL48@P0: The limited processing resources imposed by real-time\nL49@P0: operation seemed to preclude dense methods in previous\nL50@P0: monocular SLAM systems, and indeed the recent availabil\u0002ity of powerful commodity GPGPU processors is a ma\u0002jor enabler of our approach in both the reconstruction and\nL51@P0: tracking components. However, we also believe that there\nL52@P0: has been a lack of understanding of the power of bring\u0002ing dense methods fully ‘into the loop’ of tracking and re\u0002construction. The availability of a dense scene model, all\nL53@P0: the time, enables many simplifications of issues with point\u0002based systems, for instance with regard to multiple scales\nL54@P0: and rotations, occlusions or blur due to rapid motion. Also,\nL55@P0: our view is that the high quality correspondence required\nL56@P0-1: by both tracking and reconstruction will always be mostrobustly and accurately enabled by dense methods, where\nL57@P1: matching at every pixel is supported by the totality of data\nL58@P1: across an image and model.\nL59@P1: 2. Method\nL60@P1: The overall structure of our algorithm is straightforward.\nL61@P1: Given a dense model of the scene, we use dense whole im\u0002age alignment against that model to track camera motion\nL62@P1: at frame-rate. And tightly interleaved with this, given im\u0002ages from tracked camera poses, we update and expand the\nL63@P1: model by building and refining dense textured depth maps.\nL64@P1: Once bootstrapped, the system is fully self-supporting and\nL65@P1: no feature-based skeleton or tracking is required.\nL66@P1: 2.1. Preliminaries\nL67@P1: We refer to the pose of a camera c with respect to the world\nL68@P1: frame of reference w as\nL69@P1: Twc =\nL70@P1: \u0012\nL71@P1: Rwc cw\nL72@P1: 0\nL73@P1: T 1\nL74@P1: \u0013\nL75@P1: , (1)\nL76@P1: where Twc ∈ SE(3) is the matrix describing point trans\u0002fer between the camera’s frame of reference and that of the\nL77@P1: world, such that xw = Twcxc. Rwc ∈ SO(3) is the ro\u0002tation matrix describing directional transfer, and cw is the\nL78@P1: location of the optic center of camera c in the frame of\nL79@P1: reference w. Our camera has fixed and pre-calibrated in\u0002trinsic matrix K and all images are pre-warped to remove\nL80@P1: radial distortion. We describe perspective projection of a\nL81@P1: 3D point xc = (x, y, z)\nL82@P1: > including dehomogenisation by\nL83@P1: π(xc) = (x/z, y/z)\nL84@P1: >.\nL85@P1: Our dense model is composed of overlapping keyframes. Il\u0002lustrated in Figure 1, a keyframe r with world-camera frame\nL86@P1: transform Trw, contains an inverse depth map ξr: Ω → R\nL87@P1: and RGB reference image Ir : Ω → R\nL88@P1: 3 where Ω ⊂ R2\nL89@P1: is the image domain. For a pixel u := (u, v)\nL90@P1: > ∈ Ω,\nL91@P1: we can back-project an inverse depth value d = ξ(u) to\nL92@P1: a 3D point x = π\nL93@P1: −1\nL94@P1: (u, d) where π\nL95@P1: −1\nL96@P1: (u, d) = 1\nL97@P1: dK−1\nL98@P1: ˙u.\nL99@P1: The dot notation is used to define the homogeneous vector\nL100@P1: ˙u := (u, v, 1)>.\nL101@P1: 2.2. Dense Mapping\nL102@P1: We follow a global energy minimisation framework to es\u0002timate ξr\nL103@P1: iteratively from any number of short baseline\nL104@P1: frames m ∈ I(r), where our energy is the sum of a photo\u0002metric error data term and robust spatial regularisation term.\nL105@P1: We make each keyframe available for use in pose estimation\nL106@P1: after initial solution convergence.\nL107@P1: We now define a projective photometric cost volume Cr for\nL108@P1: the keyframe as illustrated in Figure 1. A row Cr(u) in\nL109@P1: Figure 1. A keyframe r consists of a reference image Ir with pose\nL110@P1: Trw and data cost volume Cr. Each pixel of the reference frame\nL111@P1: ur has an associated row of entries Cr(u) (shown in red) that\nL112@P1: store the average photometric error or cost Cr(u, d) computed\nL113@P1: for each inverse depth d ∈ D in the inverse depth range D =\nL114@P1: [ξmin, ξmax]. We use tens to hundreds of video frames indexed as\nL115@P1: m ∈ I(r), where I(r) is the set of frames nearby and overlapping\nL116@P1: r, to compute the values stored in the cost volume.\nL117@P1: the cost volume (called a disparity space image in stereo\nL118@P1: matching [14], and generalised more recently in [10] for\nL119@P1: any discrete per pixel labelling) stores the accumulated pho\u0002tometric error as a function of inverse depth d. The aver\u0002age photometric error Cr(u, d) is computed by projecting a\nL120@P1: point in the volume into each of the overlapping images and\nL121@P1: summing the L1 norm of the individual photometric errors\nL122@P1: obtained:\nL123@P1: Cr(u, d) = 1\nL124@P1: |I(r)|\nL125@P1: X\nL126@P1: m∈I(r)\nL127@P1: kρr (Im, u, d) k1 , (2)\nL128@P1: where the photometric error for each overlapping image is:\nL129@P1: ρr (Im, u, d) = Ir (u) − Im\nL130@P1: \nL131@P1: π\nL132@P1: \nL133@P1: KTmrπ\nL134@P1: −1\nL135@P1: (u, d)\nL136@P1: \u0001\u0001 . (3)\nL137@P1: Under the brightness constancy assumption, we hope for\nL138@P1: ρ to be smallest at the inverse depth corresponding to the\nL139@P1: true surface. Generally, this does not hold for images cap\u0002tured over a wide baseline and even for the same viewpoint\nL140@P1: when lighting changes significantly. Here, rather than using\nL141@P1: a patch-based normalised score, or pre-processing the input\nL142@P1: data to increase illumination invariance over wide baselines,\nL143@P1: we take the opposite approach and show the advantage of\nL144@P1: reconstruction from a large number of video frames taken\nL145@P1: from very close viewpoints where very high quality match\u0002ing is possible. We are particularly interested in real-time\nL146@P1: applications where a robot or human is in the reconstruc\u0002tion loop, and so could purposefully restrict the collection\nL147@P1: of images to within a relatively narrow region.\nL148@P1: In Figure 2, we show plots for three reference pixels where\nL149@P1-2: the function ρ (Equation 3) has been computed and aver-Figure 2. Plots for the single pixel photometric functions ρ(u) and the resulting total data cost row C(u) are shown for three example\nL150@P2: pixels in the reference frame, chosen in regions of differing discernibility. Pixel (a) is in a textureless region and not well localisable; (b)\nL151@P2: is within a strongly textured region where a point feature might be detected; and (c) is in a region of linear repeating texture. While the\nL152@P2: individual costs exhibit many local minima, the total cost shows clear a clear minimum in all except nearly homogeneous regions.\nL153@P2: aged to form C(u) (Equation 2). It is clear that while an\nL154@P2: individual data term ρ can have many minima, the total cost\nL155@P2: generally has very few and often a clear minimum. Each\nL156@P2: single ρ is a simple two view stereo data term, and as such\nL157@P2: has no useful information for scene regions which are oc\u0002cluded in the second view. As noted in [13], while increas\u0002ing signal to noise ratio, using many views with a robust L1\nL158@P2: norm enables the occlusions to be treated as outliers, while\nL159@P2: increasing the chance that a region has at least one useful\nL160@P2: non occluding data term.\nL161@P2: Shown in Figure 3, an inverse depth map can be extracted\nL162@P2: from the cost volume by computing arg mind C(u, d) for\nL163@P2: each pixel u in the reference frame. It is clear that the esti\u0002mates obtained in featureless regions are prone to false min\u0002ima. Fortunately, the sum of individual photometric errors\nL164@P2: in these regions leads to a flatter total cost. We will there\u0002fore seek an inverse depth map which minimises an energy\nL165@P2: functional comprising the photometric error cost as a data\nL166@P2: term and a regularisation term that penalises deviation from\nL167@P2: a spatially smooth inverse depth map solution.\nL168@P2: 2.2.1 Regularised Cost\nL169@P2: We assume that the inverse depth solution being recon\u0002structed consists of regions that vary smoothly together with\nL170@P2: discontinuities due to occlusion boundaries. We use a regu\u0002lariser comprising a weighted Huber norm over the gradient\nL171@P2: of the inverse depth map, g(u)k∇ξ(u)k\u000f. The Huber norm\nL172@P2: is a composite of two convex functions:\nL173@P2: kxk\u000f =\nL174@P2: (\nL175@P2: kxk\nL176@P2: 2\nL177@P2: 2\nL178@P2: 2\u000f\nL179@P2: if kxk2 ≤ \u000f\nL180@P2: kxk1 −\nL181@P2: \u000f\nL182@P2: 2\nL183@P2: otherwise\nL184@P2: (4)\nL185@P2: Within k∇ξrk2 ≤ \u000f an L\nL186@P2: 2\nL187@P2: 2 norm is used, promoting smooth\nL188@P2: reconstruction, while otherwise the norm is L1 forming the\nL189@P2: total variation (TV) regulariser which allows discontinu\u0002ities to form at depth edges. More specifically, TV allows\nL190@P2: discontinuities to form without the need for a threshold\u0002specific non-convex norm that would depend on the recon\u0002struction scale which is not available in a monocular setting.\nL191@P2: In this case \u000f is set to a very small value ≈ 1.0e\nL192@P2: −4\nL193@P2: to reduce\nL194@P2: the stair-casing effect obtained by the pure TV regulariser.\nL195@P2: As depth discontinuities often coincide with edges in the\nL196@P2: reference image, the per pixel weight g(u) we use is:\nL197@P2: g(u) = e\nL198@P2: −αk∇Ir(u)k\nL199@P2: β\nL200@P2: 2 , (5)\nL201@P2: reducing the regularisation strength where the edge mag\u0002nitude is high, thereby limiting solution smoothing across\nL202@P2: region boundaries. The resulting energy functional there\u0002fore contains a non-convex photometric error data term and\nL203@P2: a convex regulariser:\nL204@P2: Eξ =\nL205@P2: Z\nL206@P2: Ω\nL207@P2: n\nL208@P2: g(u)k∇ξ(u)k\u000f + λC (u, ξ(u)) odu . (6)\nL209@P2: In many optical flow and variational depth map methods,\nL210@P2: a convex approximation to the data term can be obtained\nL211@P2: by linearising the cost volume and solving the resulting\nL212@P2: approximation iteratively within a coarse-to-fine warping\nL213@P2: scheme that can lead to loss of reconstruction detail. If the\nL214@P2: linearisation is performed directly in image space as in [13],\nL215@P2: all images used in the data term must be kept increasing\nL216@P2: computational cost as more overlapping images are used.\nL217@P2: Instead, following the large displacement optic flow method\nL218@P2: of [12] we approximate the energy functional by coupling\nL219@P2: the data and regularisation terms through an auxiliary vari\u0002able α : Ω → R,\nL220@P2: Eξ,α =\nL221@P2: Z\nL222@P2: Ω\nL223@P2: n\nL224@P2: g(u)k∇ξ(u)k\u000f +\nL225@P2: 1\nL226@P2: 2θ\nL227@P2: (ξ(u) − α(u))2\nL228@P2: + λC (u, α(u)) odu .\nL229@P2: (7)\nL230@P2: The coupling term Q(u) = 1\nL231@P2: 2θ\nL232@P2: (ξ(u) − α(u))2serves to\nL233@P2: drive the original and auxiliary variables together, enforc\u0002ing ξ = α as θ → 0, resulting in the original energy\nL234@P2: (6). As a function of ξ, the convex sum g(u)k∇ξ(u)k\u000f +\nL235@P2: Q(u) is a small modification of the TV-L\nL236@P2: 2\nL237@P2: 2 ROF image\nL238@P2-3: denoising model term [11], and can be efficiently opti\u0002mised using a primal-dual approach [1][16][3]. Also, al\u0002though still non-convex in the auxiliary variable α, eachFigure 3. Incremental cost volume construction; we show the current inverse depth map extracted as the current minimum cost for each\nL239@P3: pixel row d\nL240@P3: min\nL241@P3: u = arg mind C(u, d) as 2, 10 and 30 overlapping images are used in the data term (left). Also shown is the regularised\nL242@P3: solution that we solve to provide each keyframe inverse depth map (4th from left). In comparison to the nearly 300 × 103points estimated\nL243@P3: in our keyframe, we show the ≈ 1000 point features used in the same frame for localisation in PTAM ([6]). Estimating camera pose from\nL244@P3: such a fully dense model enables tracking robustness during rapid camera motion.\nL245@P3: Q(u) + λC (u, α(u)) is now trivially point-wise optimis\u0002able and can be solved using an exhaustive search over a\nL246@P3: finite range of discretely sampled inverse depth values. The\nL247@P3: lack of coarse-to-fine warping means that smaller scene de\u0002tails can be correctly picked out from their surroundings.\nL248@P3: Importantly, the discrete cost volume C, can be computed\nL249@P3: by keeping the average cost up to date as each overlapping\nL250@P3: frame from Im∈I(r) arrives removing the need to store im\u0002ages or poses and enabling constant time optimisation for\nL251@P3: any number of overlapping images.\nL252@P3: 2.2.2 Discretisation of the Cost Volume and Solution\nL253@P3: The discrete cost volume is implemented as an M × N × S\nL254@P3: element array, where M × N is the reference image res\u0002olution and S is the number of points linearly sampling\nL255@P3: the inverse depth range between ξmax and ξmin. Linear\nL256@P3: sampling in inverse depth leads to a linear sampling along\nL257@P3: epipolar lines when computing ρ.\nL258@P3: In the next section we will use MN × 1 stacked rasterised\nL259@P3: column vector versions of the inverse depth ξ and auxiliary\nL260@P3: α variables (see [16] for more details of similar schemes).\nL261@P3: d is the vector version of ξ; and a is the vector version